 if you see differences in the  supermarket's organic and traditional or  conventional varieties are different  varieties usually and so maybe you can  tell the difference but normally you  can't tell the difference and the  industry has worked  hard to make it so because informing  consumers that every week is a new  variety it's very challenging it's the  same in strawberry so strawberries are  very regionally adapted so the varieties  that grow in California don't grow well  here the ones in Florida don't grow well  here are varieties don't grow well in  Southern California and you think about  the vast differences in climate you can  imagine that maybe these you know we  don't see the same trees growing in  different places will vary too the same  way you have to have varieties that are  adapted to where you live and where you  the climate and soils that you have  we've done a little bit with  blackberries and we're doing more with  blackberries because there is growing  availability and growing demand for  people to have blackberries and the  local market has been very sketchy for  us because of winter blackberries are  not particularly winter hardy and so now  we're working on varieties that can grow  in New York so that we can have local  blackberries as well most people don't  think New York when they think  strawberries four six in California  which of course 95% of the strawberries  in the US come from California but the  Northeast is the biggest region for very  consumption when you talk to companies  like Driscoll's the Northeast is their  market they love to sell in an or thesis  people like berries and they eat a lot  of them but that's at least four days  like truck to get here so the berries in  New York for strawberry start right now  so if you're interested in getting fresh  local strawberries now is the time to  start going to the farmers market they  are the first fruit to hit the market so  growers it's really important for  growers in New York they start getting  people to come out to the farms to come  to the stands to see some fresh product  and of course strawberries a trail of  strawberries right so it's a great way  to start the summer in New York  strawberries are the third leading fruit  crop so it is an important crops for  farmers in New York as well nationwide  were the sixth leading producer of  strawberries of course California  produces about five times more than the  rest of the country combined so six is  still a little bit down but California  has  much peaked in their production acreages  actually started to go down because they  just don't have any more space for  strawberries there's some labor issues  so we are looking for the possibility of  growing more strawberries in other parts  of the country including the Northeast  as strawberries do very well here  they're native there are strawberries  that grow while people have a tradition  of growing while are collecting wild  strawberries and raspberries of course  they're very small so it's a little  better to get them from a farmer so you  can pick them a little easier so of  course in the supermarket you have big  red raspberries big red strawberries of  course I have an interest in producing  big red ones as well this is one of our  varieties that is a big red raspberry  prince and giant fruit size and some of  these can and can be the size of a small  strawberry which is unusual but it's  really nice for pictures because it  fills containers really fast  people don't understand this as well so  berries are what are called filled  packed the berries you get in the  container in the supermarket we're put  in the container by the person who  picked it from the plant and no one else  touches it after that so how fast you  can get them into the container really  makes a difference for both the picker  and for the grower because they don't  have to pay as much to get them  harvested so of course we like big red  ones but competing with California big  red berries is most likely gonna be a  losing proposition their climate is so  perfect for growing berries that we need  to have some way to distinguish  ourselves from the California berries  you see in the supermarket and how we do  that generally is with flavor and  quality so this is our again another big  red berry but this is our Archer  strawberry that came out last year what  we find it's a little bit softer than  what you get in the supermarket because  we don't have to drive for days in a  semi to get it to market and that makes  a big difference both in quality and in  flavor because we can pick them much  riper and they don't have to be so firm  I get a lot of complaints people tell me  oh the strawberries in the supermarket  they're so hard they don't taste like  anything  that's part of the modern system of if  you want to drive it across the country  from central Mexico in December you've  got to have some firmness to get it  there and so we develop varieties that  are not that firm they're a little  juicier you have more flavor and they  can get to market in the same day often  so they really are very fresh and so our  growers can differentiate themselves by  having a product that tastes better and  my colleagues at Driscoll's that I know  very well some of them actually are my  student former students they tell me in  the Northeast when June berries or  strawberries are coming in June in New  York their sales go down and that is  their peak season as well and if a  company like Driscoll's can notice that  people are buying local produce it  really it does open their eyes and  they're you know the market is there  because people are interested in having  a good quality product that is locally  grown one of the things we're doing is  working with protected agriculture so  you can see on the top are what we call  high tunnels these are about twelve to  fourteen feet high on the bottom we call  low tunnels they go over the strawberry  plant it's just to keep the rain off so  a raspberry plant a raspberry fruit  that's been rained on lasts about a day  to two days and you're in your  refrigerator because it has absorbed  some of that water and really softened a  raspberry that's never been rained on  can last seven to ten days in your suit  in your refrigerator so the difference  between two days and seven days is huge  when you're talking to shelf-life or a  product so just keeping the plants dry  and keeping the fruit dry it's a big big  advantage of course this year as the  rains have not stopped for us for the  most part it really has made a big  difference and we've just started  harvesting strawberries and normally in  a year like this when they've been  rained on 10 or 15 times bungle bungle  load gets really high the strawberries  are nice and dry under there  they're clean we haven't had to spray  fungicides and so everything it's  looking really good so this is one of  the things we've worked with my  colleague Marvin Pritz has also worked  on this quite a bit to show growers in  New York that we can grow strawberries  and raspberries and very high-quality by  doing some production practices  a little different than that we're used  to pretty much even in California the  raspberries are grown under tunnels to  keep them dry and so now with this kind  of technology we can have fresh berries  in New York somewhere being grown from  about mid-may until mid-november to  early December and people don't realize  that you can continue to get local  berries well into the fall and some of  the growers actually have problems  people don't believe them they're  picking raspberries in October and  they're putting them on the market and  they they can't believe that they  actually came from the local field and  they have to educate the grower there or  the consumer and show them pictures of  the field hood and the fruit being  picked raspberries are an interesting  crop because big and red is what you see  in the supermarket but what we would  like to do and what we're starting to do  is expand that palette to get people to  think about raspberries beyond that the  industry has been focused much more in  the last 10 to 15 years on having them  available all the time pretty much now  though you go to the supermarket  strawberries raspberries blackberries  blueberries they're there all the time  year-round and now's the time where we  can expand that and start giving people  some different choices the big red ones  are always going to be there so now  let's look at specialty ones that come  around seasonally ones that taste a  little different of course there's  yellow blush black purple like I was  saying and we have varieties like that  and some of the flavors and those are  just outstanding right now where you're  going to find those are in the local  markets because some of the growers want  to differentiate themselves now from the  other local growers and have a product  that's a little different so we've  developed a few varieties in recent  years for crimson night were - - that  dark red color you can imagine the juice  and jam that you make out of something  like this it's really outstanding the  frozen product there is a really nice it  has a really sweet flavor the blush one  we call that apricot in the breeding  world but the best-tasting raspberry  that I have it really is confuses people  because it never turns red when you  freeze it it turns pink it's very  interesting variety but it's really  interesting to the growers as well  because even if people don't buy it they  come over to their market just to see it  or to try it and then they might just  buy red ones but it starts a  conversation with the grower and the  consumer so they can start to learn  about this and we see this very  interesting to the the gardening world  the home gardener as well because they  know more about it because they've been  growing it themselves and you know I  grew up on a farm where we always had a  garden and gardeners like to brag to  each other about whatever it is they  have that their neighbor doesn't have  and so this kind of stuff really brings  a conversation about what they might be  growing so this is our we have a purple  strawberry as well it ripens to a red  wine color the juice is just as dark as  you see on the outside we're selling  that to Burpee because the homeowners  are really interested something  different and the flavor on that is just  just outstanding you won't see that in a  supermarket probably for a while because  nobody knows how to market it exactly  but but the home gardener already knows  because they know what tastes good and  so that's what they're looking for now  people don't realize that strawberries  can come in other colors there's white  strawberries as well or starting to see  those in the marketplace a little bit  this is a pine berry type you might see  that occasionally if you ever go on  YouTube and look at white strawberries  in Japan you'll be amazed at what  they're doing it's it's a little it's a  little crazy they're selling them  individually individual berries and  they're packed in a nice soft container  and they sell them as gifts and special  occasions it's pretty wild what they're  what what people will do if they have  too much money on their hand but growers  are looking to sell and make money so  you know what if you can have a  specialty product why not  and with that conclude again I will tell  you now is the time to get your  strawberry so get to the local markets  and I think you'll be really really  pleased with what what you can get  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu

