 it's just to keep the rain off so  a raspberry plant a raspberry fruit  that's been rained on lasts about a day  to two days and you're in your  refrigerator because it has absorbed  some of that water and really softened a  raspberry that's never been rained on  can last seven to ten days in your suit  in your refrigerator so the difference  between two days and seven days is huge  when you're talking to shelf-life or a  product so just keeping the plants dry  and keeping the fruit dry it's a big big  advantage of course this year as the  rains have not stopped for us for the  most part it really has made a big  difference and we've just started  harvesting strawberries and normally in  a year like this when they've been  rained on 10 or 15 times bungle bungle  load gets really high the strawberries  are nice and dry under there  they're clean we haven't had to spray  fungicides and so everything it's  looking really good so this is one of  the things we've worked with my  colleague Marvin Pritz has also worked  on this quite a bit to show growers in  New York that we can grow strawberries  and raspberries and very high-quality by  doing some production practices  a little different than that we're used  to pretty much even in California the  raspberries are grown under tunnels to  keep them dry and so now with this kind  of technology we can have fresh berries  in New York somewhere being grown from  about mid-may until mid-november to  early December and people don't realize  that you can continue to get local  berries well into the fall and some of  the growers actually have problems  people don't believe them they're  picking raspberries in October and  they're putting them on the market and  they they can't believe that they  actually came from the local field and  they have to educate the grower there or  the consumer and show them pictures of  the field hood and the fruit being  picked raspberries are an interesting  crop because big and red is what you see  in the supermarket but what we would  like to do and what we're starting to do  is expand that palette to get people to  think about raspberries beyond that the  industry has been focused much more in  the last 10 to 15 years on having them  available all the time pretty much now  though you go to the supermarket  strawberries raspberries blackberries  blueberries they're there all the time  year-round and now's the time where we  can expand that and start giving people  some different choices the big red ones  are always going to be there so now  let's look at specialty ones that come  around seasonally ones that taste a  little different of course there's  yellow blush black purple like I was  saying and we have varieties like that  and some of the flavors and those are  just outstanding right now where you're  going to find those are in the local  markets because some of the growers want  to differentiate themselves now from the  other local growers and have a product  that's a little different so we've  developed a few varieties in recent  years for crimson night were - - that  dark red color you can imagine the juice  and jam that you make out of something  like this it's really outstanding the  frozen product there is a really nice it  has a really sweet flavor the blush one  we call that apricot in the breeding  world but the best-tasting raspberry  that I have it really is confuses people  because it never turns red when you  freeze it it turns pink it's very  interesting variety but it's really  interesting to the growers as well  because even if people don't buy it they  come over to their market just to see it  or to try it and then they might just  buy red ones but it starts a  conversation with the grower and the  consumer so they can start to learn  about this and we see this very  interesting to the the gardening world  the home gardener as well because they  know more about it because they've been  growing it themselves and you know I  grew up on a farm where we always had a  garden and gardeners like to brag to  each other about whatever it is they  have that their neighbor doesn't have  and so this kind of stuff really brings  a conversation about what they might be  growing so this is our we have a purple  strawberry as well it ripens to a red  wine color the juice is just as dark as  you see on the outside we're selling  that to Burpee because the homeowners  are really interested something  different and the flavor on that is just  just outstanding you won't see that in a  supermarket probably for a while because  nobody knows how to market it exactly  but but the home gardener already knows  because they know what tastes good and  so that's what they're looking for now  people don't realize that strawberries  can come in other colors there's white  strawberries as well or starting to see  those in the marketplace a little bit  this is a pine berry type you might see  that occasionally if you ever go on  YouTube and look at white strawberries  in Japan you'll be amazed at what  they're doing it's it's a little it's a  little crazy they're selling them  individually individual berries and  they're packed in a nice soft container  and they sell them as gifts and special  occasions it's pretty wild what they're  what what people will do if they have  too much money on their hand but growers  are looking to sell and make money so  you know what if you can have a  specialty product why not  and with that conclude again I will tell  you now is the time to get your  strawberry so get to the local markets  and I think you'll be really really  pleased with what what you can get  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu


